<poml>
    <task>
        Generate a fleet health report: run the single-log health check-set across every
        log under a directory (or an explicit list of sources), then aggregate the results
        into one ranked table and a fleet-level verdict.
        <stepwise-instructions>
            <list listStyle="decimal">
                <item>
                    Enumerate the logs. If given a directory, list its files with whatever
                    file-listing tool is available (Bagel has no directory-listing tool of
                    its own) - one file per candidate log, skipping obvious non-log files
                    (metadata sidecars, `.gitignore`, index files already implied by a
                    `metadata.yaml` next to split `.db3`/`.mcap` parts). If given an explicit
                    list of sources, use it as-is.
                </item>
                <item>
                    For each candidate, call `describe_data_source`. If it errors (wrong
                    extension for the resolved format, missing required `args` such as a
                    CAN `dbc` or a Betaflight `log_index`), try the fix the error message
                    suggests once; otherwise record that log as UNREADABLE with the one-line
                    reason and move on - one bad file must never abort the fleet report. A
                    directory of mixed formats (PX4 + ArduPilot + ROS2 side by side) is the
                    normal case, not an error.
                </item>
                <item>
                    For every log that did describe successfully, run the `diagnose/robot_health`
                    methodology: inventory topics, run only the checks the log's topics
                    support (power, IMU/vibration, GPS, data gaps, errors/status), and get a
                    verdict and 1-2 key numbers per check - discover-and-adapt to each
                    log's own topic names exactly as that capability describes, since two
                    logs in the same directory may be different ecosystems entirely. Keep
                    this per-log pass concise: capture the worst verdict and its headline
                    number, not the full per-log report card - the fleet table is the
                    deliverable, not N repeated single-log reports.
                </item>
                <item>
                    Aggregate into the fleet table (see output format): one row per log,
                    ranked worst-first (❌ before ⚠️ before ✅ before UNREADABLE, since an
                    unreadable log is a data problem rather than a robot-health verdict;
                    ties broken by earliest offending timestamp, then by log id).
                </item>
                <item>
                    Every numeric claim, in every row, must trace to a query actually run
                    against that log - the same house rule as `diagnose/robot_health`. Do
                    not infer a fleet-wide number (e.g. "average voltage") by re-deriving it
                    from the printed table; if the user wants a fleet-wide aggregate,
                    compute it from the underlying per-log numbers explicitly.
                </item>
            </list>
        </stepwise-instructions>
    </task>

    <hint>
        Fleet table skeleton - ranked worst-first, one row per log:
        <code lang="text">
            Fleet health: ./data/logs - 3 logs, 2026-08-01 to 2026-08-03

            Log            Duration  Worst   Headline
            forklift_042   8m12s     ❌ GPS   fix&lt;3 for 41% of the log, 12s dropout at t=210s
            forklift_017   6m03s     ⚠️ IMU   accel_z variance 3.1x baseline at t=90s
            forklift_005   5m50s     ✅       all checks clear
            forklift_009   -         ⛔       unreadable: missing dbc arg for CAN capture

            VERDICT: 4 logs: 1 healthy, 2 need attention, 1 unreadable - forklift_042 needs
            the GPS dropout investigated first.
        </code>
    </hint>

    <output-format>
        A compact fleet report, in this exact shape:
        <list listStyle="decimal">
            <item>
                One header line: the directory (or source count) and log count, plus the
                date/time range spanned if it's cheap to compute from what was already
                fetched.
            </item>
            <item>
                One fleet table, one row per log, columns: log id (filename or a short
                identifying path segment), duration, worst verdict (icon plus the check
                name it came from, or a plain ✅ with no check name when every check that
                ran passed), and a one-line headline number for that worst verdict. An
                UNREADABLE log gets a `⛔` row with the one-line reason instead of a
                verdict.
            </item>
            <item>
                Rows ranked worst-first: ❌, then ⚠️, then ✅, then ⛔ last.
            </item>
            <item>
                A final `VERDICT:` line: total log count, a healthy/needs-attention/unreadable
                breakdown, and the single log + item that most needs attention first (or
                stating all-clear if every readable log passed every check that ran).
            </item>
        </list>
        Do not repeat each log's full single-log report card; the table row is the summary.
        Offer to run `diagnose/robot_health` on a specific log if the user wants its full
        report card.
    </output-format>
</poml>
